Step 1: Log in on the serial SetUp port as an Administrator-level account
        (factory default: super / super) and reach the Text Interface command
        prompt. Keep this serial session open until Step 7 succeeds.

Step 2: Open the TACACS Parameters menu
        /N [Enter]              Network Parameters (eth0/IPv4 [Shared])
        28 [Enter]              TACACS Parameters -- the item number varies by
                                model/firmware; pick the entry labelled "TACACS"

Step 3: Set each item (type the item number, [Enter], then the value)
         1. Enable                     : On
         2. Primary Host/Address       : ${SERVER_IP}
         3. Secondary Host/Address     : (leave Undefined -- single tacquito server)
         4. Secret Word                : ${SECRET}
         5. Fallback Timer             : 15
         6. Fallback Local             : ${FALLBACK_LOCAL}
         7. Authentication Port        : 49
         8. Default User Access        : Enable On, Access Level ViewOnly,
                                         Service Access Telnet/SSH On   (REQUIRED; see notes),
                                         Port Access: the serial ports User- and
                                         ViewOnly-level logins may reach (factory: none;
                                         see "Port access" below); on a power unit also
                                         Plug Access and Plug Group Access
         9. Account Management Module  : Enabled   (TACACS+ authorization -- carries priv-lvl back; REQUIRED)
        10. Session Management Module  : Enabled   (TACACS+ accounting -- needs tacctl's tacquito patch 0002)
        11. Service Name               : ${SERVICE_NAME}     (factory default is "wti"; see notes below)
        12. Debug                      : Off       (turn On only while troubleshooting)

Step 4: Ping test
        13. Ping TACACS Servers        -- confirms ${SERVER_IP} answers ICMP from the unit.
                                          ICMP only: a pass does NOT prove TCP/49 works (Step 5)

Step 5: Check the unit's firewall (IP Tables under /N)
        If the IP Tables list sets 'iptables -P INPUT DROP' or ends in
        'iptables -A INPUT -j DROP', it must also accept loopback and replies
        to the unit's own connections, BEFORE the final DROP:
          iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
          iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
        (older builds: -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED). Without them the
        unit's TACACS+ SYN leaves but tacquito's SYN-ACK is dropped: every
        login waits out the Fallback Timer and tacquito logs nothing.

Step 6: Save
        Press [Esc] repeatedly until the unit prints "Saving Configuration"
        and returns to the command prompt.

Step 7: Test from a SECOND session -- keep the Step 1 serial session open as a
        back door until this works. Log in with a tacquito user that is a member
        of scope '${SCOPE}', forcing the password method first:
          ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=password <user>@<wti-ip>
        then type /H: the Help menu lists only the commands allowed for the
        access level the server assigned (see the mapping below). Log out with
        /X so the accounting STOP record is sent.
        If a plain 'ssh <user>@<wti-ip>' is closed without a password prompt
        while the password method works, the unit's Invalid Access Lockout is
        armed from earlier failures: /UL clears it.

Step 8: Only if Step 7 fails -- turn on the unit's TACACS debug
        /N, 28, then 12. Debug: On, [Esc] to save. Retry the Step 7 login: the
        unit now echoes each TACACS+ exchange (PAP authentication, then the
        authorization that returns priv-lvl, then accounting) on THIS serial
        session. Read it next to tacquito's debug log (see "Verify" below),
        then set 12. Debug back to Off and [Esc] to save.
        Session dropped ("Broken pipe") right after login, no accounting STOP:
        the tacquito binary lacks patch 0002 -- run 'tacctl upgrade', or set
        10. Session Management Module: Disabled until it is rebuilt.
